## Reference: Subagent prompt — closed-lost extraction

Spawn one `general-purpose` subagent per batch, all in one turn. Replace `{{BATCH}}` and `{{OUT}}` with the batch input path and output path. Keep everything else verbatim; each line below exists because its absence cost a prior run time or accuracy.

> You are re-deriving the reason a set of closed-lost Nitra sales deals actually died, using PRIMARY SOURCE data from Salesforce (including Gong call recordings/summaries logged as Salesforce Task records).
>
> **INPUT:** `{{BATCH}}` — a JSON array of deals, each with: practice, opportunity, oppId (Salesforce Opportunity Id), accountId, amount, closeDate, sheetLossReason, and optionally sheetHypothesis / sheetDeathMoment / sheetNotes from an earlier analysis (treat those as hypotheses to check, never as evidence).
>
> **COMPANY CONTEXT:** Nitra sells a Visa credit card for independent medical practices (~2.2% cashback on medical/healthcare spend), a spend-management/AP platform (QuickBooks integration), and "NitraMart", a medical supply marketplace with 5-10% back on select injectables (e.g. Avastin). Heavily ophthalmology/retina. Competitors named in deals: Amex, Capital One, Chase, WEX, Corpay, Ramp, Bill.com/Divvy, Comdata, PNC. Purchasing channels/GPOs: Besse Medical, McKesson (incl. OnMark Vision GPO), CuraScript SD, AmerisourceBergen/Cencora, Cardinal Health, Henry Schein, Medline, Biocare, Ally GPO; Medivant, Turbare and Northmark are Avastin compounders/repackagers.
>
> **STEP 1** — Pull primary sources via `mcp__cc5af9f6-21f2-4dda-afce-989ccc5c69d6__soqlQuery`:
> (a) Opportunity fields, batched ~12 oppIds per query:
> `SELECT Id, Name, Amount, StageName, CloseDate, CreatedDate, Description, NextStep, LeadSource, Owner.Name, Account.Name, AccountId FROM Opportunity WHERE Id IN ('...','...')`
> (b) Activity history, 3-5 oppIds per query:
> `SELECT WhatId, AccountId, Subject, ActivityDate, Type, Description FROM Task WHERE WhatId IN ('...','...') ORDER BY ActivityDate DESC`
> If an opportunity has zero Task rows, query by AccountId instead and note "account-level activity only" in the death moment.
> Gong records have Subjects starting "[Gong"; Descriptions contain "Call brief:", "Key Discussion Points:", "Next steps:" — the richest evidence. "Reached gatekeeper"/"reached voicemail" records carry no product signal but DO count as evidence of an access problem.
>
> **IMPORTANT:** `Task.Description` is NOT filterable with LIKE in this org. Do not try. For keyword searches use SOSL via `mcp__cc5af9f6-21f2-4dda-afce-989ccc5c69d6__find`.
>
> **CRITICAL** — check that activity falls BEFORE the opportunity's CloseDate. Earlier analyses quoted calls that postdated the close by 6-23 months, from later re-prospecting cycles or sibling records. If the only activity postdates the close, the honest hypothesis is "no contact on record before close." Opportunities created 2025-08-30 with earlier close dates are Pipedrive backfills — expect exactly this.
>
> Watch for records that are not real losses: duplicates (created seconds apart, identical amounts), test/error records ("ENTERED WRONG Account"), accounts that later signed up or have a Closed Won sibling, added EINs under an existing customer. Flag these plainly in contradictionNote and set stallStage honestly.
>
> **CONTEXT MANAGEMENT:** Task Descriptions run 1-3k chars. Never pull all deals' activity at once. Work in small chunks. If a tool result is auto-saved to a file for being too large, use `mcp__workspace__bash` with jq/python to extract only the fields you need. Do not hold raw activity text for the whole batch in context.
>
> **STEP 2** — For each deal write:
> - `hypothesis` — 1 sentence (max ~150 chars), the real reason the deal died, specific and concrete. Name the actual blocker: competitor and rate, named vendor, specific missing feature, who blocked it. If sources show only unanswered outreach, say so and quantify ("14 calls, never past the gatekeeper").
> - `deathMoment` — 2-3 sentences (max ~420 chars) on the last meaningful interaction and what killed it. Quote or closely paraphrase real language and include the date. Evidence, not speculation.
> - `stallStage` — one of: "no contact", "gatekeeper only", "champion engaged", "champion demo", "DM demo", "verbal/late stage". A demo must be evidenced by a real meeting, not a 1-minute unanswered call.
> - `gapTags` — 0-3 from EXACTLY: `["dm-access","multi-ein","cashback-rate","accounting-integration","switching-cost","vendor-acceptance","gpo-lockin","trust","float-terms","emr-conflict","not-icp","champion-turnover","tax-treatment","no-engagement"]`. Tag only what the sources show blocked the deal — a feature the prospect was interested in is not a cause of death.
> - `evidenceStrength` — "strong" | "moderate" | "weak"
> - `contradictsOld` — true if your read materially differs from sheetHypothesis when one was supplied; if none was supplied, compare against sheetLossReason (the SFDC picklist) instead — a deal filed as "Not a Priority" that actually died on a competitor rate is a contradiction worth surfacing
> - `contradictionNote` — one short sentence if contradictsOld, else ""
> - `gongLink` — the first https://…gong.io/call?id=… URL found in the evidence, else ""
>
> **HONESTY REQUIREMENT:** Never invent detail or attribute a quote not in the data. Thin sources → evidenceStrength "weak" and a hypothesis stating only what is knowable. "Only two logged voicemails; no substantive contact on record" is a better answer than a plausible-sounding objection.
>
> **OUTPUT:** Write the file with `mcp__workspace__bash` (python3 json.dump) — the Write/Edit file tools cannot reach `/sessions/...` paths. Write a JSON array to `{{OUT}}` — one object per input deal, same order, with exactly these keys: practice, opportunity, oppId, hypothesis, deathMoment, stallStage, gapTags, evidenceStrength, contradictsOld, contradictionNote, gongLink. Validate: `python3 -c "import json;d=json.load(open('{{OUT}}'));print(len(d))"` must print the input count.
>
> Return ONLY a compact summary under 200 words: count processed, stallStage and evidenceStrength distributions, contradictsOld count, top gapTags, 2-3 most notable corrections, and any non-loss/duplicate records found. Do NOT paste the JSON.

