## Reference: Reason tags, product-vs-access split, and re-engagement score

### The 14 reason tags

Each closed-lost deal carries 0-3 tags. A tag is applied only when the sources show that thing actually blocked the deal — not when it was merely mentioned or listed as a feature of interest. (The first pass over-counted `multi-ein` six-fold by treating a "Features Valued" entry as a cause of death.)

| Tag | Label in deliverables | Applies when… | Class |
|---|---|---|---|
| `no-engagement` | Never got a substantive conversation | No stakeholder ever heard a real pitch: voicemails, deferrals, silence | access |
| `dm-access` | Decision-maker never reached | A champion engaged, then relayed the pitch second-hand to doctors/procurement who were never on a call | access |
| `champion-turnover` | Champion left or was reassigned | The internal advocate resigned, was promoted, or left mid-cycle | access |
| `switching-cost` | Switching / change-management cost | Doctors or admins declined to disturb working card/AP workflows (Amex points, per-doctor cards, mid-flight migrations) | mixed |
| `cashback-rate` | Incumbent rate beats or matches 2.2% | A named competitor rate was at or above Nitra's, or rebate breadth lost | product |
| `gpo-lockin` | GPO lock-in on drug spend | A GPO/distributor agreement governs purchasing and the card has nothing to run on | product |
| `accounting-integration` | AP / accounting integration depth | Lost on 3-way PO matching, 1099s, Sage/NetSuite support, reconciliation | product |
| `vendor-acceptance` | Key vendor won't take the card | Dominant supplier is ACH-only, or NitraMart doesn't carry the drug | product |
| `float-terms` | Float, APR and payment terms | Wanted longer payoff windows, higher limits, or APR matching | product |
| `multi-ein` | Multi-EIN / decentralized purchasing | Multi-entity structure that couldn't be accommodated — and this is what stalled it | product |
| `emr-conflict` | EMR migration or EMR-bundled card | EMR go-live froze the decision, or the EMR vendor offered its own card | product |
| `tax-treatment` | Cashback tax treatment | CPA or doctors objected to cashback as taxable income vs Amex points | product |
| `trust` | Trust — unproven vendor | Unproven-vendor risk, or a bad prior experience with Nitra | mixed |
| `not-icp` | Structurally not ICP | Bankruptcy, hospital-owned, lost the providers whose spend the case rested on | neither |

**Product blocker** = any of the product-class tags. **Access only** = any access-class tag and no product-class tag. `switching-cost` and `trust` are counted in neither bucket by default; the deliverable states that. This split is the single most important number in the executive summary because it separates what a roadmap can fix from what a process change can fix.

### Stall stage

Furthest point the deal demonstrably reached, from activity evidence:

`no contact` → `gatekeeper only` → `champion engaged` → `champion demo` → `DM demo` → `verbal/late stage`

"Champion demo" and "DM demo" must be evidenced by a real meeting record, not a 1-minute unanswered call that someone logged as a demo (this happened in the source sheet).

### Evidence strength

- `strong` — direct quotes about the blocker, dated
- `moderate` — some signal, partly inferred
- `weak` — little or no source material; the hypothesis says only what is knowable

### Re-engagement score (derived, 1-5)

Assigned by `scripts/merge_batches.py` when no Salesforce field is supplied. The rule is intentionally simple so it can be explained on one line of the Read Me:

```
start at 2
+1 if stallStage in {champion demo, DM demo, verbal/late stage}
+1 if amount >= $1,000,000
+1 if any tag in {cashback-rate, dm-access, float-terms, switching-cost}   # addressable by a play we control
-1 if evidenceStrength == weak
-1 if any tag in {not-icp, trust}
-1 if stallStage in {no contact, gatekeeper only}
-2 if not a genuine loss / duplicate / asked not to be contacted
clamp to 1..5
```

Why these levers: proximity to close and deal size set the prize; an addressable blocker means a rep can actually change the outcome; weak evidence means we don't know enough to prioritise it; not-ICP and trust rarely reverse; a deal nobody ever spoke to is a fresh prospect, not a re-engagement. Calibrated on the July-2026 population: 15 deals at 5, 72 at 4 (35% warm), which is a queue a team can actually work. The score orders the queue; it does not predict revenue — say that in the Read Me.

If John supplies a Salesforce field name, read it, skip the rule, and note the source.

### Customers mode: adoption state

`fully ramped` · `partially adopted` · `onboarding` · `stalled/low usage` · `at risk` · `unknown`

"At risk or stalled" = `at risk` ∪ `stalled/low usage`. Risk signals are free text; the workbook flags any non-empty value. Don't derive a numeric score for customers — the adoption state plus the named risk is more useful to a CSM than a number.

