## Reference: QA'ing a large deck without LibreOffice timing out

A one-slide-per-deal deck runs 100-300 slides. LibreOffice takes 60-120 s to convert that to PDF, which exceeds the 45 s shell timeout, and a half-finished conversion leaves a lock file that makes the next attempt hang forever.

### Build a QA subset instead

`scripts/build_lost_deck.js` accepts `--qa` which emits only: title, executive summary pages, key actions, first reasons page, first overview page, and six deal slides chosen to stress the layout (rank 1, the longest practice name, the longest death moment, one weak-evidence deal, one with a contradiction note, the last deal). That deck is ~15 slides and converts in seconds.

```bash
node scripts/build_lost_deck.js final.json qa_subset.pptx --qa
cp qa_subset.pptx /tmp/qa/ && cd /tmp/qa
python <pptx-skill>/scripts/office/soffice.py --headless --convert-to pdf qa_subset.pptx
pdftoppm -jpeg -r 110 qa_subset.pdf s
```

Copy the JPEGs back under `outputs/` before viewing them — the `Read` tool can't see `/tmp`.

### What to look for

- Practice names over ~45 characters wrapping out of the header band (the builder scales the font; confirm it worked)
- Death-moment text spilling past its card — the card height is computed from text length; a source string with unusual line breaks can defeat it
- The facts card's last row touching the card border
- Footer colliding with a chart or table on the summary pages
- Rank badges on the reasons pages continuing across pages (1-3, 4-6, …)

### If you must convert the full deck

Detach it so the shell timeout doesn't kill it, and poll:

```bash
setsid nohup python <pptx-skill>/scripts/office/soffice.py --headless --convert-to pdf full.pptx > /tmp/conv.log 2>&1 < /dev/null & disown
sleep 40; ls -la full.pdf || echo pending
```

If it never appears, `pkill -9 -f soffice`, remove any `.~lock.*#` files in the working directory, and try again from a fresh directory.

### Validation is separate from rendering

`validate.py` runs in seconds on any size deck and catches the XML faults PowerPoint rejects. Run it every time; rendering is for layout, validation is for file integrity.

